{
  "title": "Coupling multi-pair tailored probe",
  "source": "3f6734b",
  "tipCheck": "At f5801c5, after the emergency-record discovery skip and the frontier fix, the test gives the same trains, approach count, completed orders, and final digest.",
  "result": "met: in the pinned run, trains on c01 and c02 are alive at the same time from tick 49483 to tick 49858, and c01 forms a second train at tick 55810 after its first train retired at tick 49858. The disabled control forms no train, and a second enabled run gives the same state digests.",
  "test": "TestCouplingNaturalMultiPairProbe in internal/sim/coupling_natural_probe_test.go, skipped in short mode. It runs the pinned run, a second enabled run, and a disabled control as parallel subtests. After each tick it checks CouplingError, the member motion oracle, CheckContract, SafetyObservation().Check(), the live train registry, and the order count: accepted orders equal submitted orders, which equal completed plus interrupted plus pending plus incomplete riders aboard. It keeps a SHA-256 digest of ExportState every 1,000 ticks and at the end.",
  "harness": "The rows come from a scratch test in package sim at 3f6734b, not in the tree. It builds the same network, fleet, and demand as the test. Each run has the coupling marker, couplingEnabled true, an all-Compact fleet, virtual platoons with a limit of 2, and no placed trips, groups, or approaches. Demand copies the session generator for the balanced pattern: PCG(seed, ^seed), one offer each 60/rate seconds after the tick, uniform origin and destination over all 8 stations, skipped when 200 orders wait. For each tick in which a front is at rest at E with a virtual follower at rest, it runs prepareCouplingApproach and records the verdict. distinctPairsAtRest counts each front and rear pair one time, by whether their routes separate at F and by the lane where the follower linked. For each approach in its wait phase with the rear at rest, it runs the reservation plan and the adoption preparation and records the result. These checks do not change the run: the pinned row has the same trains as the test.",
  "siteRule": "As in the natural multi-pair record. E is the start of the first cell of the assembly lane that holds the junction resource of M. On each 120 m assembly lane that is cell 2, so E = 60 m. The assembly site is [E-30, E+40], with rear staging E and front staging E+12. The split site starts 1 m after the end of the first cell of the split lane (31 m), is 49 m long, and has rear staging at its start plus the boundary margin plus 0.5 m and front staging 12 m later. ValidateCouplingGeometry accepts all 4 corridors. project.Validate, through cmd/projectcheck, accepts the full project: 8 stations, 80 berths, 320 nodes, 420 lanes, 70 pods, the coupling marker and sites, and balanced demand.",
  "network": {
    "name": "Square ring with corner spokes",
    "shape": "A one-way square ring with 1,200 m sides. At each corner a spoke continues the incoming side straight on, and the ring turns left. The spoke has a 280 m approach lane, the 120 m assembly lane p-M, and the 120 m split lane M-F. These lanes are collinear. The assembly and split lanes are Compact-only. At F, a left and a right branch leave at 45 degrees. Each branch has one station with 10 berths on a side loop, and the station entry is 300 m past F. The left branch returns to the next ring side. The right branch returns past the spoke, joins M at about 20 degrees on a 3 m/s lane, crosses the spoke, and goes to the next ring side. All other lanes have 14 m/s.",
    "turnedLink": "The spoke continues the ring side straight on, so a link that forms on that side, on the approach lane, or on the assembly lane has no turn while both members go into the spoke. The run of a link ends at F when the members go to different branches. When they go to the same branch and the link reaches the 45-degree fork lane, the link turns, so prepareCouplingApproach refuses the pair. A link that ends more than 300 m (platoonHorizon) before the fork stays straight; the findings give that case. That refusal is correct, because these members share the route after the split.",
    "thirdPod": "Only the orders for the two stations of a spoke use that spoke, so each assembly lane carries about one quarter of the orders. A third pod is still the main refusal (see approachOutcomes).",
    "exitClosure": "The members of an accepted pair go to different branches at F, so the exit closure of bindExits ends on the two fork lanes. The station entries are 300 m past F, past the closure.",
    "oneAttempt": "A pod passes one corridor on each journey, so the single attempt for each journey does not take a second corridor from it.",
    "stopAtE": "A pod that crosses M on the slow join lane holds the junction of M for some seconds. A spoke pod that arrives during the crossing stops at E, the start of the conflict zone of M on the assembly lane. The next spoke pod queues behind it, links on the assembly lane, and stops.",
    "demand": "8 orders each simulated minute, balanced, 70 pods placed round robin on the 8 stations. The fleet cannot serve all orders, so the pending queue grows during each run."
  },
